The NHS states that "Melatonin is available on prescription only. It comes as standard tablets, slow release tablets, capsules and a liquid that you drink." NHS North West Knowledge Now states that "Melatonin is classified as a medicine in the UK and is only available on prescription. In contrast, it is readily available to purchase in some countries, e.g., USA." North East London shared care guidelines state that Circadin® "is licensed in the UK for the short term treatment of primary insomnia characterised by poor quality sleep in adults aged 55 years and over," and that "There are no licensed products of Melatonin in the UK for the treatment of childhood insomnia."

On its role, East London NHS Foundation Trust describes melatonin as "a pineal hormone which affects sleep," whose "main function is the regulation of circadian rhythm and sleep." The NHS states that synthetic melatonin "adds to your body's natural supply of melatonin, so you fall asleep more quickly and you're less likely to wake up during the night."

No UK source in the evidence named specific foods that contain melatonin. No UK source gave a dietary reference intake or a dietary upper limit. The maximums listed below are prescribing doses from a medical-treatment context. No page from the BNF was found.

