The sources provided do not include the US figure of 600 IU, so this summary cannot confirm or compare it directly. The NHS states that "children from the age of 1 year and adults need 10 micrograms (mcg) of vitamin D a day," including pregnant and breastfeeding women and people at risk of vitamin D deficiency. The NHS page does not give an IU equivalent for this 10 microgram figure, although it does express its upper limits in both units, such as 100 micrograms (4,000 IU). The NHS also states that from about late March or early April to the end of September, the majority of people should be able to make all the vitamin D they need from sunlight on their skin.

On its role, NHS 111 Wales states that vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, and that these nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy. A Health Research Authority summary describes vitamin D as a fat-soluble vitamin that functions as a hormone to regulate calcium metabolism. East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust states that it also has a role in immune function and cell division. These statements come from local or regional NHS organisations, not from the main nhs.uk page, NICE or the BNF.

On food sources, an expert paper hosted by NICE (written by Cancer Research UK for guidance PH32) states that vitamin D is found in only a few foods. It names fatty fish and fish oils, liver, meat and eggs as the main natural sources, and notes that in the UK some milks, margarine and breakfast cereals are often fortified. Local NHS sources also list oily fish, cod liver oil, egg yolk, red meat, offal, fortified spreads, yoghurts, cereals and some mushrooms.

The NHS states an upper limit of 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) a day for adults and children aged 11 to 17. No NHS, NICE or BNF source in the evidence states vitamin D's UK legal category. Local NHS prescribing documents distinguish vitamin D food supplements from licensed medicines.

