The NHS states that "Adults need 10 micrograms (400 International Units or IU) of vitamin D a day." The sources in this report do not include a US figure, so the 600 IU value cannot be confirmed or compared here.

The NHS states that "Vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body," and that "These nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy." It also states that "Children from the age of 1 year and adults need 10 micrograms (mcg) of vitamin D a day," and that this includes pregnant and breastfeeding women.

For food sources, an expert paper by Cancer Research UK hosted on the NICE website names fatty fish and fish oils, liver, meat and eggs as the main natural sources. The same paper notes that in the UK, processed and some powdered milks, margarine and breakfast cereals are often fortified. Local NHS bodies list similar foods, and University Hospitals Coventry and Warwickshire NHS Trust states that "Sunshine, not food, is where most of your vitamin D comes from."

The NHS gives a maximum of 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) a day for adults and 50 micrograms (2,000 IU) a day for children aged 1 to 10 years. No national UK source was found on the legal category of vitamin D products.

